WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
SPEAKER 1: The
implications of God

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.340
are in the hierarchy of
things, including us,

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.420
and in the imperfection, even of
the highest things that we see.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.890
Why would a place
devoted to learning,

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:21.990
to the exercise of the mind,
to rational understanding also

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:23.130
be interested in faith?

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.400
Aren't they contradictory?

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.540
And that I will address
at a little bit of length

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.110
because that would be why
we study Genesis here.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.190
Philosophy is the
love of wisdom.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.410
And wisdom is an achievement
of the intellect,

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.860
of what you can know by
thinking things through.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.100
We kind of start with
the things in front of us

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.690
and try to understand how
we know what they are.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.690
Same thing when one of
the things in front of us

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.550
is each other, ourselves.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:52.900
What are we?

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.260
And so you start with questions.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.977
Socratic philosophy begins
this way with, what are we,

00:00:57.977 --> 00:01:01.120
how should we live, what are
these other things we see,

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.230
and how do we know
what they are?

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.980
How do we figure it out?

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.480
And there's a great account of
that in classical philosophy.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.020
And classical
philosophy, of course,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.860
was born in a time before
the Christian faith,

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:17.915
in pagan times, 400 or
so years before Christ.

00:01:17.915 --> 00:01:19.290
And their account
runs like this.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.870
They say, well, when
you start thinking

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.360
about how you recognize things,
you're asking the question,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.880
how do you use common nouns?

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.700
Down there, there's a
glass which they put here

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.950
for me in case I get raspy.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.320
And I'll hold it up so
you can see it, all right?

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.800
And if you think about
it, that's one glass.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:40.520
It's a kind.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.980
There are many kinds--
many, many kinds.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.270
And somehow, we
always recognize them.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:50.180
And the fact that we can
use the common noun "glass"

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.330
is the central operation,
according to the classics,

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:54.050
of human reason.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:57.140
It's the thing we can do that
the other creatures can't do.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.630
And yet, when we realized
that about ourselves

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.070
and look inside
ourselves, we see

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:04.770
that whatever it is about
us that makes us different,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.470
that thing, too, is imperfect.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:10.979
First of all, it's located
in a perishable body.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:13.800
And second of all,
we have this thing

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.220
that is about us, about the way
we learn and the way we think--

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.040
that's the reason why
students get dark circles

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.570
under their eyes at finals time.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.120
We go from one thing to the
next thing, to the next thing.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.670
And we have to piece them
together while we go.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.580
We don't know
everything at once.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.030
We can't really see anything
except what we can see.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.790
Aristotle says, "There's
nothing in the soul that doesn't

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.180
get there through the senses."

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.070
So that's a kind of a limit.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:45.040
Well, once you see what
those problems are,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.980
you can imagine a being that
knows everything in an instant,

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.440
sees everything in an instant.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.040
The implications of God are
in the hierarchy of things,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.840
including us, and in
the imperfection, even

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.610
of the highest
things that we see.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.940
And that means
that the humanities

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.410
raise the question of God.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.210
And so you need to be
thinking about that.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:12.610
But then, think about this.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.940
How are you going
to know about God?

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:19.200
Because if you imagine a perfect
being, it would be omnipotent.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.320
That means it could do anything.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.540
And then, on the other
hand, to be omnipotent,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.820
it would need to be omniscient.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.730
It would need to
know everything.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.910
And if it was the
origin of things,

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.750
it would be the creator.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.410
Now, I will tell you
that in Greek philosophy,

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.930
they don't imagine a
creator because they

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.070
stick with what they see.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.080
And they see that the
planets are always there.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.038
And they always move in
the patterns they move.

00:03:45.038 --> 00:03:47.970
And grown up dogs give
rise to baby dogs.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.500
And acorns produce oak trees.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.550
And so the implication is
that it's gone on forever.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.220
But on the other hand,
they did ask the question,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.110
did all this start somewhere?

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.990
And, if so, where?

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.960
And if there actually
was a creator, then

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.860
he would actually have
to be outside nature.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.650
He wouldn't be just
that dense lump

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.660
at the beginning of things.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.850
He'd have to be something
outside that thing

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:18.050
to be the source of it.

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:22.640
And he would need to be an
eternal thing, a thing that

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.570
didn't start--

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.560
in order to be
perfect, I'm saying.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.880
And so then you start
drawing a picture of God.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:37.940
And that picture is
supported by the traditions

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.800
of learning that come to us,
especially through Judaism

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.500
and Christianity.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.230
In Christianity,
"Jesus" is the word.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.840
It's what God has to
say, just like we say.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:52.710
Use words.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.880
And only we use them.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.480
And so there's an
invitation in the faith

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.740
to think and try to explain,
understanding that we're

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:05.130
unlikely to be able to explain
everything at once, including,

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.620
especially, the
thing of which we

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:12.570
can have no sensory evidence,
the creator of everything.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:16.180
And that's why,
in the tradition,

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.850
the study of God,
theology, has always

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:21.340
been part of the canon
of the liberal arts

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.460
and fits in the
canon, understanding

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.045
that the faithful part of
it, the revealed part of it,

00:05:28.045 --> 00:05:32.230
that that part is a different
kind of knowing than the kind

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:36.190
that we undertake when
we observe and think

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:39.700
and record and calculate
and, in the natural sciences,

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.425
experiment.

00:05:41.425 --> 00:05:43.550
And it doesn't mean that
they're not both valuable,

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.360
but they are different.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.370
And so to call yourself a
wholly thoughtful people,

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:51.020
as far as a human
being could be--

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.530
person-- wholly
thoughtful person,

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.010
you'd have to do some
thinking about God.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:00.430
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you for watching.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.400
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlights series

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.310
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:06.510
online courses.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:10.670
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

